User Interface Requirements:

- The BPMN canvas must be the primary focus of the application and occupy the maximum available screen space.
- Avoid permanently visible left and right side panels.
- Use collapsible, resizable, or floating panels instead of fixed sidebars.
- The BPMN diagram area should consume at least 80-90% of the screen width when panels are collapsed.
- Allow users to show or hide:
  - Issue Panel
  - Properties Panel
  - Process Explorer
  - Validation Results

Canvas Requirements:

- Large infinite canvas with zoom and pan support.
- Automatically fit the BPMN diagram to the available screen size.
- Provide a "Fit to Screen" button.
- Provide a "Center Diagram" button.
- Support full-screen BPMN editing mode.
- Minimize unused whitespace.

Issue Panel Requirements:

- Display the Issue Panel as:
  
  - A collapsible drawer.
  - A floating window.
  - A bottom dock panel.

- Do not reserve permanent screen space for the Issue Panel.

- Allow users to open the panel only when required.

- Clicking an issue should navigate to and highlight the affected BPMN object.

Visual Design Requirements:

- Use a light professional theme by default.
- Avoid black or dark backgrounds unless the user explicitly enables Dark Mode.
- Use a clean white or light-gray canvas background.
- Ensure high contrast and readability.
- Use subtle shadows and borders instead of heavy dark colors.
- Maintain a modern enterprise appearance similar to BPMN.io, Signavio, or Visio.

Layout Requirements:

- Prioritize diagram visibility over tool panels.
- Automatically maximize available canvas area.
- Keep toolbars compact and unobtrusive.
- Hide advanced options until requested.
- Ensure large processes remain readable without excessive scrolling.

Goal:

The BPMN diagram should always be the center of attention, with supporting tools appearing only when needed. The user should feel like they are working on a large professional modeling canvas rather than inside a dashboard with multiple fixed panels.